---
title: Pad Thai
servings: 3
time required: 40 minutes
cuisine: Thai
image: https://hot-thai-kitchen.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/pad-thai-blog.jpg
source: https://hot-thai-kitchen.com/best-pad-thai/
---

Boil @water in a #pot and then turn off heat. Add @dry rice noodles{240-300g} and let soak in warm water until cooked, about ~{5%minutes}.

Strain noodles and set aside.

Heat a #wok or large nonstick skillet{} over high heat and add just enough @oil{1%tbsp} to coat the bottom. Sear @chicken breast{1%lbs}(chopped) until done and remove from pan. Season your chicken with @?chili flakes{}, @?salt{}, @?pepper{}, @?garlic{}, etc. as desired here.

In the same wok, now over medium heat, add @oil{1%tbsp}, and then add @garlic{9%cloves}(chopped), @chili flakes{}(to taste), and @onion{1}(diced). Sauté for a few minutes, until the garlic starts to turn golden and the onion is translucent. If the wok looks dry, add a little more oil.

Turn heat up to high and then add @peanut butter{1%tbsp} and @pad thai sauce{4.5%tbsp}(Mae Ploy brand is recommended). Mix, ensuring to keep from burning with water or oil.

Add noodles and additional @pad thai sauce{4.5%tbsp}. Keep tossing until all the sauce is absorbed.

Once sauce is absorbed, you can turn off the heat and taste the noodles for doneness. If they're still undercooked, add a little more water and continue cooking.

Once noodles are done, turn heat back to medium and push them to one side of the pan. Add a little extra @oil{1%tbsp} to the empty space and add @eggs{2}.

Break the yolks, then put noodles on top of eggs and cook for about 30 seconds. Flip and toss to mix eggs into noodles.

Toss the cooked protein back in, plus any collected juices. 

Add @?bean sprouts{2.5%cups}, @?garlic chives{70g}(cut into 2-inch pieces), and @?roasted peanuts{1/8%cup}(roughly crushed).

Turn off the heat and toss until well mixed.

Serve immediately with a @?lime wedge{} and @?roasted peanuts{1/8%cup}(roughly crushed) on top. 